Z||000000||Pheagey||11-16-2001||07:39 AM||||Io heard that the front rims are 6" and the back 7" wide on '88's, is this correct? If it is can I use 6" all around?<BR>thanks..<BR>(Soon to have wheels again).<BR><P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed.<BR>"The car formerly know as Dj's Fiero."||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||11-16-2001||09:14 AM||||6 inch would be the min. but bigger would have to with the size of tire.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||11-16-2001||11:33 AM||||88 coupes used the same 14" Tech rims all the way around.  Only the Formula and GT's had different size wheels front and rear, 15x6 front and 15x7 rear.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnF||11-16-2001||11:45 AM||||It's amazing what I learn from this forum.   <P>Now for the dumb question of the week - <P>How do you rotate the tires (assuming it's done) with uneven wheel widths from front to rear?<P>Is this correct only for '88?  <P>||||reg||5
Z||000004||DRH||11-16-2001||12:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B>It's amazing what I learn from this forum.   <P>Now for the dumb question of the week - <P>How do you rotate the tires (assuming it's done) with uneven wheel widths from front to rear?<P>Is this correct only for '88?  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>You just go side to side unless you have directional tires, then you don't rotate them unless you take them and have them remounted. I don't think the $$$ to do that would justify any extra wear you MIGHT get by rotating.<P>The rim width difference only applies to 88 GT/Formula. If you have factory spec tires you will have the same problem on 86-87 GTs, tey have 205/60/15 front and 215/60/15 rear even though they use the same size rims.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||JohnF||11-16-2001||12:41 PM||||Thanks for the reply.  <P>I guess, then, I'm glad I have an '88 coupe.<P>||||reg||12
